# What p < 0.05 really means
subtitle: "A blood-pressure trial on one curve: what a p-value measures, the misreading that flips it, and why 0.05 is only a convention."
thumb: graph

## What p < 0.05 really means
use: distribution_curve
with:
    mean_label: '0'
    sd_label: ' SE'
    shade:
      from: 1.96
      side: two
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 175
      w: 1000
      h: 530
    show_sd_ticks: false

    arrow obs #b5482e from.x 757.5 from.y 360 to.x 757.5 to.y 205 hidden true
    text l_obs "drug: 5 mmHg lower" #b5482e at.gap 10 at.offset [70,0] at.ref obs at.side above hidden true
    math wrong tex "P(\\text{no effect} \\mid \\text{data}) = 1.2\\%" x 230 y 480 hidden true
    math correct_reading tex "P(\\text{data} \\mid \\text{no effect}) = 1.2\\%" colors {"1.2":"#b5482e"} x 790 y 480 hidden true
    portrait portrait_ronald_fisher x 130 y 615 width 110 of "Ronald Fisher" hidden true
    path mt0 #3a3530 points [[180,198],[180,186]] hidden true
    text ml0 "−6" x 180 y 166 font_size 24 hidden true
    path mt2 #3a3530 points [[285,198],[285,186]] hidden true
    text ml2 "−4" x 285 y 166 font_size 24 hidden true
    path mt4 #3a3530 points [[390,198],[390,186]] hidden true
    text ml4 "−2" x 390 y 166 font_size 24 hidden true
    path mt6 #3a3530 points [[495,198],[495,186]] hidden true
    text ml6 "0" x 495 y 166 font_size 24 hidden true
    path mt8 #3a3530 points [[600,198],[600,186]] hidden true
    text ml8 "2" x 600 y 166 font_size 24 hidden true
    path mt10 #3a3530 points [[705,198],[705,186]] hidden true
    text ml10 "4" x 705 y 166 font_size 24 hidden true
    path mt12 #3a3530 points [[810,198],[810,186]] hidden true
    text ml12 "6" x 810 y 166 font_size 24 hidden true
    text unit "1 SE = 2 mmHg" #8f887c x 850 y 615 font_size 24 hidden true
    text dir "lower on drug →" #8f887c x 850 y 580 font_size 24 hidden true
    group mmaxis members ["mt0","ml0","mt2","ml2","mt4","ml4","mt6","ml6","mt8","ml8","mt10","ml10","mt12","ml12","unit","dir"] hidden true
    text c5r "2.5%" #8f887c x 799.5 y 250 font_size 24 hidden true
    text c5l "2.5%" #8f887c x 190.5 y 250 font_size 24 hidden true
    group c5 members ["c5r","c5l"] hidden true
    polygon ptail #b5482e points [[757.5,216.58532292270132],[766.8625,212.812629554573],[776.2249999999999,209.71227523060884],[785.5875000000001,207.18750174109869],[794.95,205.1499113413944],[804.3125,203.52015072493123],[813.675,202.2281294254508],[823.0374999999999,201.2128664009587],[832.4,200.4220556542269],[841.7625,199.81143446599432],[851.125,199.34402761687159],[860.4875,198.98932917338044],[869.8499999999999,198.72247112806212],[869.8499999999999,198],[757.5,198]] style.fill #b5482e style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    polygon ptail2 #b5482e points [[232.5,216.58532292270132],[223.1375,212.812629554573],[213.77500000000003,209.71227523060884],[204.41249999999997,207.18750174109869],[195.05,205.1499113413944],[185.6875,203.52015072493123],[176.325,202.2281294254508],[166.96250000000003,201.2128664009587],[157.60000000000002,200.4220556542269],[148.2375,199.81143446599432],[138.875,199.34402761687159],[129.51250000000005,198.98932917338044],[120.15000000000003,198.72247112806212],[120.15000000000003,198],[232.5,198]] style.fill #b5482e style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.6 hidden true

    card title "What p < 0.05 really means"

    hide shade_label

What does p below 0.05 actually tell you? It is research's most common verdict, and its most misread. This curve shows what it does and doesn't say.
    draw axis curve

A blood-pressure drug is tested against placebo. If the drug did nothing, the gap between the groups would still wobble around zero, by chance.
    draw mean_line

With this trial's size, the wobble has a standard error of 2 millimetres. Most results land within two of these steps.
    draw mmaxis

Before the trial, mark the five percent most extreme results a useless drug could produce: beyond 1.96 steps, 2.5 percent in each tail.
    draw shade shade2 c5

The trial finds blood pressure 5 millimetres lower on the drug: 2.5 standard errors from zero, inside the shaded region.
    draw obs l_obs

    pause 0.4

    erase c5

The p-value is the area beyond the result itself: if the drug did nothing, results this extreme would come up 1.2 percent of the time.
    group ptails members ["ptail","ptail2"]
    text plab "p = 1.2%" #b5482e x 875 y 290 font_size 30

Both tails count, because a drug raising pressure by 5 would have been just as surprising. That is what two-sided means.
    highlight ptail2

So the p-value is the chance of data this extreme, given no effect.
    draw correct_reading

The common misreading flips it: a 1.2 percent chance that the drug does nothing, given the data.
    draw wrong

That is a different number. It depends on how likely the drug was to work before the trial, which the p-value never uses.
    morph wrong tex "P(\\text{no effect} \\mid \\text{data}) \\ne 1.2\\%"

Nor does it measure size: a trial of many thousands could make a trivial half-millimetre drop just as significant.
    highlight obs

The 0.05 line itself is a convention.
    highlight shade shade2

Ronald Fisher suggested it in 1925 as a convenient limit for judging a result, not a law.
    draw portrait_ronald_fisher

    erase portrait_ronald_fisher

    quiz "A trial reports p = 0.012. Which statement is right?"
        right: If the drug did nothing, results this extreme would occur 1.2 percent of the time. The p-value assumes no effect; it cannot give the chance of no effect, or its size.
        * There is a 1.2% chance the drug does nothing
        + If the drug did nothing, results this extreme would occur 1.2% of the time
        * The drug lowers blood pressure a lot
